DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ggrreeppcc__cc(1)  is  a  driver  for  ggrreeppcc(1).  It prints PCRE patterns that
       ggrreeppcc(1) will use to search for declarations, definitions, and/or  uses
       of _p_a_t_t_e_r_n in C source code.

       _p_a_t_t_e_r_n  is a PCRE pattern.  It normally represents a C identifier, and
       word boundaries are implicitly added to it in most cases.

   TTyyppeess ooff ccooddee
       This program can produce PCRE pattern for several types of  code.   The
       following  arguments can be passed to the --tt option to select the types
       of code that will be searched.

       ee      enum constant definitions.

       ff      Function prototypes and definitions.  This can be further subdi‐
              vided into:

              ffpp     Function prototypes.

              ffdd     Function definitions.

              ffll     Linux kernel system calls.  This can  be  further  subdi‐
                     vided into:

                     ffllpp    Linux kernel system call prototypes.

                     fflldd    Linux kernel system call definitions.

              ffgg     glibc internal names of libc functions.  This can be fur‐
                     ther subdivided into:

                     ffggpp    glibc prototypes.

                     ffggdd    glibc definitions.

       mm      Macro definitions.

              mmff     Function‐like macro definitions.

              mmoo     Other macro definitions.

       tt      Type definitions.

       uu      Uses.   Searches  occurences  of  _p_a_t_t_e_r_n within enums, function
              bodies, macro replacements, and type definitions, and prints the
              whole enum, function, macro, and/or type definitions.   In  this
              case  there are no word boundaries set by default, so if _p_a_t_t_e_r_n
              is ooppeenn, ffooppeenn will also be found; that’s to allow searching for
              patterns containing any sequence of characters.

              This can be further subdivided into:

              uuee     Uses within enum definitions  (except  as  an  enum  con‐
                     stant).

              uuff     Uses within function definition bodies.

              uumm     Uses within macro replacements.

              uutt     Uses within type definitions (except enum; that is: type‐
                     def, struct, or union).

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --tt _t_y_p_e
              Restrict  the  search  to  a specific _t_y_p_e of code (see Types of
              code under DESCRIPTION).  This option  can  be  passed  multiple
              times to search for various types of code.  Default: ee ff mm tt.

